15. COPYRIGHT REGISTRATION AND OWNERSHIP VERIFICATION

This document records intended licensing terms. It is not a copyright registration certificate, government-issued instrument, legal opinion, or independent proof of ownership.

Before submitting a copyright registration application or making a formal ownership claim, the responsible applicant should:

1. Identify the specific work or works for which protection or registration is sought.
2. Identify the author, current rights holder, and relevant chain of ownership.
3. Review employment, contractor, agency, and contributor agreements.
4. Separate original works from third-party and open-source components.
5. Preserve dated source files, drafts, version history, publication records, and relevant agreements.
6. Prepare the application, copies of the work, declarations, and supporting materials required by the applicable procedure.
7. Verify current official requirements and fees.
8. Submit the application through the relevant official registration process.
9. Retain submission records and any subsequent correspondence.
10. Update internal records only when the relevant status is supported by documentary evidence.

A website may contain multiple works and components requiring separate legal consideration.

Copyright protection, ownership, eligibility for registration, and evidentiary requirements depend on the work, the facts, and applicable law. Registration must not be represented as completed until the relevant process has actually been completed.

15.1 Official reference resources

For official information in India, consult:

* Copyright Office: https://copyright.gov.in/
* Intellectual Property India: https://ipindia.gov.in/

These links are provided for reference. They do not imply that MedOrbits has filed or obtained any registration.

